<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Flat black, so the launcher's own mask supplies the disc.

  The source artwork is a finished round icon: an opaque black circle with the
  mark on it, already masked. Putting that whole image into the *foreground*
  layer would have the launcher mask it a second time, and the mark would end up
  both cropped and shrunk. So the black is the background layer, where a mask is
  exactly what is wanted, and only the mark is a foreground.

  ## Sizing the foreground, and why it is not a round number

  The mark PNGs are cut from the artwork at a scale that preserves the artwork's
  own proportion of mark to disc, rather than at some "safe" constant. Two
  things had to be pinned down for that to be possible:

    - The launcher's visible disc is 36dp in radius within the 108dp viewport.
      Measured off the device rather than assumed, because a launcher is free to
      choose and this one does not use the whole canvas.

    - The artwork's own ratio, measured as the mark's furthest pixel from the
      disc centre, divided by the disc radius.

  Mark radius = ratio x 36. For the current artwork the ratio is 0.682, giving a
  24.5dp mark.

  An earlier version hardcoded 31dp, which is inside the 33dp safe circle and so
  looked defensible, but it produced a mark/disc ratio of 0.861 against the
  artwork's 0.804, which is visibly larger in the launcher than in an image
  editor, because the editor shows the whole canvas while the launcher shows only
  the masked disc. Deriving the scale from the artwork is the only way that
  cannot drift.

  If the artwork is replaced, re-measure the ratio rather than reusing 24.5.
-->
<shape xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:shape="rectangle">
    <solid android:color="@android:color/black" />
</shape>
